A||||9||86 SE||torsion bar||||1||
Z||000000||86 SE||06-11-2000||01:52 AM||||Do fieros have a torsion bar? Ive heard that you could lower it this way. Is this true?||||reg||1
Z||000001||BN Boomer||06-11-2000||02:14 AM||||Fieros do not have torsion bar suspensions. They have coil springs, which can be lowered by either cutting by a reasonably knowledgeable individual who knows what he is doing or replaced with aftermarket versions.||||reg||1
Z||000002||TOM||06-11-2000||02:19 AM||||There is torsional springs on the trunk lid but I dont think tweaking these will help.||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||06-11-2000||08:42 AM||||Cutting the stock springs to lower your car is not recommended. Use stiffer lower springs or you could have problems bottoming out, which is very bad if you're cornering.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierospeeder||06-12-2000||02:02 AM||||my vw beetle has torsion bars,<BR>one way to notice if a car has torsion bars is if the car doesn't have coil springs, then it must either have torsion bars or U springs, that you see on mostly on old cars,trucks and vans.<P>the torsion bar, is either a bar, or a circular rod, that is held in place in one end, and the other is connected to your suspension.  The torsion bar, acts as a spring by a twisting motion.||||reg||1
Z||000005||lawrence||06-12-2000||12:13 PM||||Actually, it's the other way around -- a spring acts like a torsion bar.<P>I have a question for batboy.  You say that cutting springs is bad, and that you should exchange them for shorter, stiffer springs or else you could bottom out.  I was curious if you had actual numbers on various springs.<P>The reason I ask is if you cut, say, an inch off of a 10" spring, you have made that spring 10% stiffer.  Do you know the spring rates of "lowering" springs or stock Fiero springs?  The only way to increase stiffness in a spring is to make the wire diameter bigger (assuming you want to keep it the same O.D. and same material).  So if a stiffer lowering spring has a larger wire diameter, then its solid height will be larger, and hence you may actually bottom out easier that way.<P>Does anyone have any info on spring rates?<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe</A>  - WebElectric Magazine||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||06-12-2000||04:05 PM||||There are some past topics where the stock spring rates were posted. I don't claim to be an expert, but I thought most Fiero front springs were mainly a linear spring rate. If this is accurate, cutting off part of the spring does not increase the spring rate, it stays the same. Another thing you're not taking into account is the heat required to cut the spring, this can cause the spring to lose some of it's...well...some of it's spring, thus making it weaker. Most aftermarket lowering springs, like the Suspension Techniques sport springs I used, are shorter, but have a higher spring rate than the stock ones in order to make up the difference. Really, I'm not the one who should be answering this question, LowCG or several others probably know much more than I do concerning this discussion.||||reg||1
Z||000007||lawrence||06-12-2000||04:54 PM||||I'm not a spring expert either, but I disagree.  Cutting the spring will increase the spring rate.  If you reduce it's length, there will be less deflection with a given load.<P>I looked at my SPEC spring book and randomly picked a compression spring.  For a .42"OD X .063" wire, a .75" long spring has a rate of 109.2 lb/in.  The next size spring (same OD and wire) is .88" long and has a rate of 90.3 lb/in.  The longer spring is softer.<P>Can you measure the wire diameter of your lowering springs?  That might tell us something.<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe</A>  - WebElectric Magazine||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||06-12-2000||10:00 PM||||The diameter of my lowered springs is a tad over 1/2 inch (I couldn't find my measuring calipers). A tad is somewhere between 1/32" and 1/16", but there is some sort of plastic coating over the springs to keep it from rusting. I'm guessing actual wire diameter is right at 0.5". I didn't measure the stock ones, but I figured that you could do that easy enough. Somebody else help me out here. Any of you spring experts please speak up, I'm over my head. All I know is that several people who have cut their springs have had problems. Again, you might be forgetting that the heat may weaken the spring. Of course this might depends on how you cut it.||||reg||1
Z||000009||lowCG||06-12-2000||10:20 PM||||I've found that if you do cut the springs down to a point where the rate is desireable(15-25% firmer)then you must add a spacer to put the ride height back close to what it was before,lower than stock maybe,but not way down on the bumstops.<BR>Cut any straight wound spring in half,and it's now twice as stiff,half as tall.<BR>I found some weld in,adjustable spacers that were making me drool in some race catalogue the other day.<BR>||||reg||1
